+++

Kubernetes won. That is a fact about industry adoption, and it is not
automatically a fact about your three servers.

There is a smaller orchestrator that has been sitting inside the Docker Engine
the whole time — scheduling, rolling updates, rollback, secrets, overlay
networking, service discovery — and most people have written it off over what
turns out to be a name collision.

This is the second of two posts about what I would use *instead of* [the k3s
platform kit](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/). The other end of that spectrum is
[three FreeBSD boxes and no orchestrator at
all](/blog/three-freebsd-boxes-instead-of-k3s/). Swarm is the middle of it, and it is
the one I think is genuinely under-rated.

::: note
**Part 5 of 7 of [Running things on your own hardware](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about building a platform on your own hardware and then arguing about whether to.

Previous: [Three FreeBSD boxes instead of a k3s cluster](/blog/three-freebsd-boxes-instead-of-k3s/)

Next: [What a game world server actually needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/)
:::

